<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>

<!--
Files UI - Unraid plugin adding a two-pane file browser as its own "Files"
tab: folder tree on the left, file list on the right.

Follows the standard community plugin format, see:
https://wiki.unraid.net/Plugin_Development

Before cutting a release: run package.sh to build the .txz, upload it to a
GitHub release, then paste the printed MD5 into the MD5 entity below.
pluginURL points at the raw URL of this file so Unraid's plugin manager can
check for updates.

Unlike stacksUI, this plugin patches nothing and hides nothing: it adds one
page and its assets under /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/filesUI/ and leaves the
built-in File Manager (Shares -> Browse) exactly where it is. That means no
/boot/config/go boot hooks are needed, and uninstalling is just removepkg.
-->

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "filesUI">
<!ENTITY author    "macmiller">
<!-- Version follows year.month.day.n - bump n for each same-day release,
     reset to 1 when the date changes. package.sh reads this value as its
     default (single source of truth) rather than generating its own. -->
<!ENTITY version   "2026.08.04.5">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/macmillernz/unraid.files.plugin/main/filesUI.plg">
<!ENTITY SRC       "https://github.com/macmillernz/unraid.files.plugin/releases/download/&version;/filesUI-&version;.txz">
<!-- MD5 of the .txz package.sh currently produces. Re-run package.sh and
     update this whenever source/ changes, or the plugin manager will
     refuse the download as corrupt. -->
<!ENTITY MD5       "605e82db49444c011c64efa6d1e51b2a">
<!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY configdir "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;" icon="files.png" min="6.12.0">

  <CHANGES>
###2026.08.04.5
- Dropped the "User Shares (no cache)" root. It was /mnt/user0, the legacy
  array-only view of the same shares, so it showed up as a second and
  usually near-empty "User Shares" next to the real one. Only useful when
  troubleshooting the mover, and Unraid's own File Manager doesn't offer it
  as a root either. /mnt/user0 paths are still accepted as a copy/move
  destination if you type one.
- The show-hidden-files button now has a visible pressed state and reports
  what it did ("Showing 2 hidden items", "No hidden items in this folder").
  It was working all along, but most folders contain no dotfiles, so
  toggling it looked exactly like a dead button.

###2026.08.04.4
- Fixed every file operation silently doing nothing. Create, delete,
  rename, copy, move, change owner and change permissions all queue a job
  for Unraid's file_manager daemon - and that daemon was never starting.
  Files.page declares Nchan="file_manager", but emhttp resolves that
  against the *plugin's own* directory, so it looked for a script this
  plugin did not ship, and the exec failed silently. Jobs piled up in
  /var/tmp/file.manager.active and nothing ran, with no error shown.
  The plugin now ships nchan/file_manager, a supervisor that keeps exactly
  one engine running - including when Unraid's built-in File Manager is
  open at the same time, which would otherwise start a second copy.
- The Files tab now reports it plainly in the status bar if the engine is
  not running, instead of every button appearing to be dead.
- package.sh and deploy.sh now mark nchan hooks executable; a 644 there
  would reintroduce exactly this failure.

###2026.08.04.3
- Fixed the divider between the preview and the file list resizing the
  folder tree instead of the preview, snapping it wide on the first drag.
  The preview had been inserted between the tree and the tree's own
  divider, so that divider measured across the preview's whole width. The
  preview now has its own divider, and each one resizes the pane on its
  immediate left. Preview width is remembered separately from tree width.
- A pane can no longer be left collapsed: the width clamp could produce an
  upper bound below the pane's minimum on a narrow window, which then won
  and stuck, including across reloads.

###2026.08.04.2
- The "New Folder" button is now just "New", and creates a file instead of
  a folder when the name you type has an extension (notes.txt, .env). The
  dialog says which one it will create as you type. Shortening the label
  also fixes it running into the Upload button.
- New file preview: selecting a file slides a preview panel out from the
  left, between the folder tree and the file list. Handles images, video,
  audio, PDFs and text/code, falls back to a metadata card with a Download
  button for anything the browser cannot render, and reports clearly when
  a video's codec is unsupported rather than showing a dead player.
  Space toggles it, arrow keys move it through the folder, Escape closes.

###2026.08.04.1
- First release. Adds a "Files" tab with a two-pane browser: lazily-loaded
  folder tree on the left, sortable file list on the right.
- Create, rename, delete, copy, move, change owner and change permissions,
  on single items or a multi-selection, with a queue for when another
  operation is already running.
- Upload (button or drag-and-drop, chunked with progress and cancel) and
  download (single file streamed with resume support, multi-selections
  and folders streamed as a zip).
- Reuses Unraid's own file-operation engine rather than reimplementing it,
  so copy/move report real rsync progress, speed and ETA, and every
  operation obeys the same /mnt and /boot restrictions the built-in File
  Manager applies.
  </CHANGES>

  <!-- Plugin code, packaged as a single txz rooted at / so upgradepkg
       extracts straight to /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/filesUI/. -->
  <FILE Name="&configdir;/packages/filesUI-&version;.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
    <URL>&SRC;</URL>
    <MD5>&MD5;</MD5>
  </FILE>

  <FILE Run="/bin/bash">
    <INLINE>
set -e
mkdir -p "&configdir;"
echo ""
echo "Files UI installed. Open the Files tab."
    </INLINE>
  </FILE>

  <!-- Uninstall. There is no plugin-owned state to preserve (settings live
       in the browser's localStorage, not on the flash drive), so this
       removes the config dir too. -->
  <FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
    <INLINE>
removepkg filesUI-&version; || true
rm -rf "&configdir;"
    </INLINE>
  </FILE>

</PLUGIN>
